  {
    "Title": "Weapon - muzzle-loading pistol",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Emison Art Museum",
    "Institution": "DePauw University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Daily Life; Military",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "4587",
    "filename": [
      "4587-weapon-muzzle-loading-pistol.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Muzzle-loading pistol, has the tamp attached below the muzzle.  Wooden handle; fittings, barrel, and tamp are all metal. If it is Japanese, it dates around 1543 because guns were outlawed early in the Edo period, and did not reappear until the Meiji era."
  },
