  {
    "Title": "Shrine on the Island of Miyajima",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Digital photograph.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Architecture; Shinto",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/2003",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "986 w x 630 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "4559",
    "filename": [
      "4559-shrine-on-the-island-of-miyajima.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Central to Shinto is the belief in divine begins (kami) which traditionally inhabit heaven and earth.  Divine status is attached to anything which is striking, elevated and beautiful or possesses outstanding qualities: in brief, anything which awakens a sense of awe.  In this way, things of natural beauty, mountains and seas, human beings, plants and animals can become gods.  Symbols of every Shinto shrine are the gateways (torii), which in their simplest form are two pillars topped by a cross beam."
  },
  {
