    "filename": [
      "4557-shinto-shrine-customs.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Left corner: Shinto priest offering prayers of celebration (norito) to the gods. Right: Fires to be used at shrines is created the natural way. Top: Miko (shrine maidens) originally played an intercessionary role between man and the dieties, reaying the divine will.  Here they assist a wedding."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Set of Trays and Tablewares",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Yoshiaki Shimizu, ed., \"Japan: The Shaping of Daimyo Culture 1185-1868\" Washington: National Gallery of Art, 1988, pl. 236.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Decorative Arts; Food",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1024 w x 656 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "4556",
    "filename": [
      "4556-set-of-trays-and-tablewares.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This set is said to have been used by Tokugawa Iemitsu, the third Tokugawa shogun.  \"The entire set is decorated with a pear-skin ground (nashiji), a gold and silver maki-e clove floral scroll, and the three-leaved hollyhock mon.  The edges of the trays are rimmed with silver, and the interiors of the bowls are finished with red laquer.\" -  Suzuki Norio in \"Japan: The Shaping of Daimyo Culture\""
  },
  {
    "Title": "Samurai surimono - limited edition print",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Emison Art Museum",
    "Institution": "DePauw University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "4555",
    "filename": [
      "4555-samurai-surimono-limited-edition-print.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Original surimono by the celebrated master Utagawa Toyohiro, done c. 1795.  Toyohiro was a noted ukiyo-e painter, printmaker and illustrator; had studied under Toyoharu, whose studio he entered in 1782.  Surimono were limited edition prints, privately commissioned and published to announce or commemorate a special event.  They are therefore quite rare, and, if their condition is good, much higher in value than prints for which there are hundreds of extant copies.  They are often richly embossed and/or gilded, as here, where there is gilding on the sword scabbard, and plum blossoms embossed and faintly visible.  This example has an unusual horizontal format for a print.  Because it was a surimono?  There do not appear to be any creases; was it rolled, originally?  Subject is a  bald samurai with shaved pate and muttonchop whiskers.  Wears blue hakama decorated with 20 cranes.  Huge sword, with some gilding on the sword scabbard.  L. hand holds an oversized square box (for sake?). R. hand is an upraised fist.  Presence of cranes (symbols of long life) and plum blossoms (blooming at the time of the lunar New Year, and also symbols of bravery) make this an auspicious image, perhaps associated with the New Year holiday."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Sakuma Shogen",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Yoshiaki Shimizu, ed., i&quotJapan: The Shaping of Daimyo Culture 1185-1868oo scr Washington: National Gallery of Art, 1988, pl. 37.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Painting; Historical Figures",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "350 w x 768 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "4554",
    "filename": [
      "4554-sakuma-shogen.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Depiction of Sakuma Shogen Sanekatsu (1570-1642) sitting in front of a bamboo screen facing his boy attendant.  A warrior who first served Toyotomi Hideyoshi (1537-1598), Sakuma then seved three successive generations of Tokugawa shoguns: Ieyasu, Hidetada, and Iemitsu."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Sake Flask",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Money Hickman et al, \"Japan's Golden Age: Momoyama\" Dallas Museum of Art, 1996, scan of image on p.217, pl. 86.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Food",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "583 w x 768 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "4553",
    "filename": [
      "4553-sake-flask.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Tokkuri, or sake flasks, were produced in great quantity by the Bizen kilns in the Momoyama period.  In this example, clean lines define the plump, barrel-shaped body, thin neck, and crisply finished mouth.  The neat, concise form, made from a relatively fine-grained clay, provides a sympathetic surface for the red diagonal streaks which resulted from shielding a vessel wrapped in rice straw from direct contact with the flames during firing.  The straw burns away, leaving the hidasuki on a background of unscorched white clay."
  },
