    "Title": "Rakuchu Rakugai - \"In and Around the City of Kyoto\"",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Emison Art Museum",
    "Institution": "DePauw University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Painting; Material Culture",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "17th century, Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "4547",
    "filename": [
      "4547-rakuchu-rakugai-in-and-around-the-city-of-kyoto.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "67 inches H. x 142 inches W. (6 panels)  The type originated in the Momoyama period, when they were presented to visiting warlords, to take home as a memento of their visit to Kyoto.  This particular example is relatively late for the type, but a good example. The iconography for this particular type of screen pairs is set, and the example follows the program for the right hand screen of the original pair, depicting the colorful floats of the Gion Matsuri (Kyoto's \"signature\" festival) in LR, and various Kyoto landmarks, like the Kiyomizudera (a temple with a veranda supported on high pilings) in  the upper right."
  },
