  {
    "Title": "Butsudan - Private Buddhist altarpiece",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Emison Art Museum",
    "Institution": "DePauw University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Sculpture; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "16th century, Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "4414",
    "filename": [
      "4414-butsudan-private-buddhist-altarpiece.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "11.5 x 5 inches overall; figure 7.75\"; base 3.75\". Made of black lacquer. This kind of Buddhist family altar may have contained memorial tablets for dead ancestors, and/or images of various deities, depending on the sect of Buddhism.  Historically, it was maintained by a family in the home, in addition to a Shinto household shrine.  Label notes that the butsudan was a gift of  S. Ogata."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Bowl",
    "Creator": "Pierre Charles L'Enfant",
    "Contributor": "The Reeves Center, American Market Room",
    "Institution": "Washington and Lee University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1000 w x 638 h, 120 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1287",
    "filename": [
      "1287-bowl.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Of rectangular form with notched corners and circular base; decorated in underglaze blue, polychrome enamels, and gold, with cell-diaper border and husk chain at the rim, narrow bands on the base, central winged figure on opposite sides holding a knotted ribbon below which is suspended an eagle emblem, floral sprays on the alternating sides, \"Fitzhugh\" border on the interior rim together with a cell-diaper band in the center framing a single floral spray.^^Donor: Mr. and Mrs. Edward H. Thompson"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Sixteen Lohan on Bodhi Leaves",
    "Creator": "Anonymous, previously attributed to Ding Yunpeng",
    "Contributor": "Colby College Museum of Art",
    "Institution": "Colby College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": " Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "4573",
    "filename": [
      "4573-sixteen-lohan-on-bodhi-leaves.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Ink, color, and gold on bodhi leaves. An album of 12 Bodhi leaf paintings, beginning with two images of paired guardian figures (the four kings), followed by eight paintings of lohan, a picture of the white-robed Guanyin, and another guardian. Although this painting is in an elaborated mount, with high-quality brocade and a Qing dynasty  imperial inscription attributing it the famed painter Ding Yunpeng (1547-1628), it is most likely a late 19th century product."
  },
