      "4325-mound-of-buddha-images.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "In front of the Jizo is an offering box in which faithful can place coins to be used to maintain various features of the Okunoin area."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Mound of Buddha images",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Buddhism; Figures; Sculpture",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.216667",
    "Longitude": "135.583333",
    "Date": "07/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "4326",
    "filename": [
      "4326-mound-of-buddha-images.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The inscription on the stone pillar says that this mound is comprised of images dedicated to the spirits of people who died without anyone who directly cared for them. Such beings are called \"muenbotoke,\" or \"deceased ones without connections.\"  So in addition to grave sites for familiar loved ones, some Japanese Buddhists have felt the need to erect memorials for those who were not fortunate enough to have someone to remember them when they died."
  },
