  {
    "Title": "Traditional Shinto wedding at Itsukushima Shrine",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "May be used only for educational, non-commercial purposes, in accord with Fair Use Policies.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Celebrations; People; Shinto",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1200 w  x  893 h, 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4198",
    "filename": [
      "4198-traditional-shinto-wedding-at-itsukushima-shrine.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The architecture of the shrine building in the background, behind the wedding couple, speaks clearly of the intermingling of elements that are Shinto and Buddhist, perhaps Chinese and Japanese, indigenous and imported.  Identify these elements and discuss what is implied by their presence in this one structure.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Traditional Shinto wedding at Itsukushima Shrine",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "May be used only for educational, non-commercial purposes, in accord with Fair Use Policies.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Celebrations; People; Shinto; Customs and Practices",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1200 w  x  734 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4199",
    "filename": [
      "4199-traditional-shinto-wedding-at-itsukushima-shrine.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The bride and groom join a small group, presumably their parents and immediate families.  The Shinto wedding ceremony is typically attended only by a small group of immediate family or very close relatives and friends.  After the ceremony there will be a reception banquet which may include a very large number of friends, co-workers, etc.  --  It is interesting to note that, with one exception, all of the women in the group are attired in traditional kimono, while all of the men (except for the groom, of course) are wearing western style clothing.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Traditional Shinto wedding",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "May be used only for educational, non-commercial purposes, in accord with Fair Use Policies.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Celebrations; People; Shinto; Customs and Practices",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "798 w  x  1200 h, 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4197",
    "filename": [
      "4197-traditional-shinto-wedding.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This young couple were married in the traditional Shinto ceremony at the famous Itsukushima shrine on Miyajima Island in the Inland Sea, near Hiroshima.  They are wearing traditional formal dress for the Shinto ceremony.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
