  {
    "Title": "Japanese Ceramics: Dish with handle, Ao-oribe ware, view 01.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Aesthetics; Ceramics; Pottery, Japanese",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1322 w  x  1125 h, 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4173",
    "filename": [
      "4173-japanese-ceramics-dish-with-handle-ao-oribe-ware-v.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Oribe ware square dish with a broad handle, from the Mino region of Gifu prefecture, in west cental Honshu, a major pottery region.  Light stoneware, probably fired in a neutral atmosphere, iron oxide brushwork decoration under a light glaze, with two corners of the piece and the handle dipped in a copper green glaze, creating the characteristic Oribe glaze pattern. (The Avery Brundage Collection, B64P34)^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Who was Oribe and why did this style of vessel come to be called \"Oribe ware?\"   What was the relationship of Oribe-ware and tea ceremony?^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Japanese Ceramics:  Fresh water jar, Iga ware.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Aesthetics; Ceramics; Pottery, Japanese; Tea Ceremony, Japanese; no-yu",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "953 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4172",
    "filename": [
      "4172-japanese-ceramics-fresh-water-jar-iga-ware.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This Fresh water jar (\"mizusashi\") is a tea ceremony vessel, an example of Iga ware, a style of vessel created in Mie Prefecture and valued highly by tea masters.  Approximately 9 or 10 inch tall, wheel thrown using a light stoneware clay body, fired in a wood fueled kiln with resulting flashing coloration and some natural ash glaze deposits.  The black lid of the jar is lacquer, rather than clay, as was frequently the case with tea vessels.  The soft clay was manipulated, probably while the piece was still on the potter's wheel, deliberately deforming the piece slightly, which has the effect of emphasizing the soft, malleable nature of the material before it is fired.^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^What aesthetic qualities does this piece share with the Raku tea bowl shown in image ecasia000372?  Why would these objects appeal to the aesthetic  preferences of a tea master in the lineage of Sen no Rikyu?  Who was Sen no Rikyu?^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Japanese ceramics:  Square bottle by Hamada Shoji",
    "Creator": "Hamada Shoji   (1894-1977)",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to e given to the IDEAS Project, to Earlham College, and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "ceramics; Pottery, Japanese",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "888 w.  x  1042 h.,  150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4171",
    "filename": [
      "4171-japanese-ceramics-square-bottle-by-hamada-shoji.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Square bottle by Hamada Shoji.  Made with slabs of clay using the press mold technique.  Glazed with the material known as \"Mashiko stone,\" a local material that, by itself, with nothing else added to it, forms a stoneware glaze.  The glaze is a saturated iron oxide glaze with the orangish-red-brown color known as \"Kaki,\" which means \"persimmon.\"  (Gift of Gaylord Hall and Roy Leeper, 2005.88)^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco CA^^Editorial assistant:  Joseph Augustin.  Collection Librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send and suggestions or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN  47374  USA.  email:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "East Asian Ceramics:  Then and Now.  04, Shino-ware Jar by Rosanjin",
    "Creator": "Kitaoji Rosanjin",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Japanese ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1097 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4170",
    "filename": [
      "4170-east-asian-ceramics-then-and-now-04-shino-ware-jar.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This Shino-ware jar was created by Rosanjin, a great Japanese ceramic artist of the first half of the 20th century.  Rosanjin, a restaurateur by profession, was an \"amateur\" potter, who bagan making pottery because he could not find ceramic pieces that he felt were what he wanted to use in his restaurant.  He often looked back to earlier traditions to find forms, glazes, techniques, and ideas from which to draw in his own modern work.  In this piece, obviously, he has referred to the tradition of shino-ware with underglaze iron brush decoration.  He has applied them to his own contemporary form, although it is interesting to note the undulations of the rim of the piece and to then look at the rims of many Momoyama and Edo period shino-ware tea ceremony bowls. The brown brushed design on this side of the piece are said to be stylized representations of pine trees; on the other side of the piece are forms suggesting birds.  The orangish areas on the surface are areas where the glaze was^^Art Institute of Chicago; Chicago, IL^^Even though Rosanjin was an icago,amateurt;&quo artist in a different sense, there was a tradtion of the role of the amateur artist in Japan.  Research the origin of the idea of the amateur artist in the arts of Japan.  Where did the tradition originate and when was it prominent in Japan?^^Editorial assistant:  Joseph Augustin.  Collection librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "East Asian Ceramics:  Then and Now.  03,  Shino-ware Ewer",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Japanese",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "17th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1053 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4169",
    "filename": [
      "4169-east-asian-ceramics-then-and-now-03-shino-ware-ewe.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Shino-ware was associated with kilns of the Mino district, near Tajimi  in Gifu prefecture, central Honshu, north of Nagoya and Seto.  Shino-ware is characterized by its glaze, which is known simply as  Shino.   It is usually a thick white glaze with a soft lustrous surface, neither matte nor glossy, and a surprising sense of tactile softness to the touch.  Often, on the rim or other ridges of a form, the color will break to a warm orangish color, hinting at a sense of the clay body under the glaze (or it may suggest other images, as with the rim of Mrs. Ota's tea bowl in the Kawabata novel, Thousand Cranes).  It is a subtle and rich glaze, one much favored by masters of tea.  Often, but not always, designs were painted on the surface of pieces before they were glazed.  These patterns, painted with an iron slip or pigment, are partially obscured and softened by the glaze over them, creating both a quiet subtlety of design and a sense of depth to the glazed surface. --  An aside about this particular piece is the difference in color of the lid of the ewer and the body of the ewer, proper, suggesting that perhaps the pieces were fired apart from one another and that, even if they were immediately side by side in the kiln, the atmosphere in the kiln (the amount of smokiness or clarity of flame) was slightly different around each of the two pieces.  A problem that will be recognized by all potters, today, just as then.  --   Russell Tyson Purchase Fund Income, 1966.332^^Art Institute of Chicago; Chicago, IL^^Why would shino-ware appeal to masters of tea ceremony?  What were the points of congruence between the qualities of shino-ware and the aesthetics of tea? --  Note: An excellent photograph of an outstanding shino style tea bowl, one named \"Furisode,\" may be viewed on the web site of the Tokyo National Museum, along with an example of an oribe style dish and other ceramics.  Go to the site at http://www.tnm.go.jp  -- in the section in the middle of the home page of the site, select the category of objects that you wish to view (in this case, \"Decorative Art\"), on the next page click on the group of items you wish to view (in this case, \"...ceramics through Momoyama\"), then click on the thumbnail image of object you wish to view.^^Editorial assistant:  Joseph Augustin.  Collection librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "East Asian Ceramics:  Then and Now.  06,  Vase by Choi Sungjae",
    "Creator": "Sungjae, Choi  (1962 -  )",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.  Artist retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "827 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "4168",
    "filename": [
      "4168-east-asian-ceramics-then-and-now-06-vase-by-choi-s.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This slab-formed bottle vase form, titled  1cMeditation-Staying V, 1d is a contemporary Korean piece.  It refers to the much earlier Korean tradition of Punch 19ong-ware, as seen in the Punch 19ong-ware Flask in this exhibit (represented here in image ecasia000359).   1cMeditation-Staying V 1d refers to the earlier tradition through its use of materials and decorative technique.  It is a form constructed of slabs ( 1csheets 1d) of clay, cut and assembled when stiff enough to be handled without deforming them [incidentally, if you look carefully at the lower portion of the piece, you can discern a line that shows that the top of the body of the piece sits on a slab that forms the bottom of the piece].  The two lugs on the shoulder of the piece are bits of soft clay added as accents after the piece was assembled (they derive from loops of clay on the shoulders of pieces, formerly functional forms, as might be used, e.g., to attach cords to tie down a piece of material to seal the mouth of a jar).  The neck of this piece, as seen from a higher point of view, is oval in shape; it could have been formed with a slab of clay or might have been thrown on a potters 19 wheel (thrown as a cylinder, then squeezed to the oval form used on the piece).  The finished form, before firing, was coated with a thick white clay slip, using a broad, coarse brush to apply the slip.  The potter 19s finger or a tool of some sort then was used to draw through the soft slip surface, revealing the dark clay body beneath the slip coating.  [Many persons will recall using a similar technique in school art classes to create drawings on  1cscratch-board. 1d]  When fired, probably in a  1csmoky 1d reduction atmosphere, the clear glaze takes on the cool tonality of reduced iron oxide and shows a slight greenish cast, similar to the effect created on traditional Punch 19ong-ware.  The imagery on the piece also refers to the earlier tradition of Punch 19ong-ware in its content and spirit.  As stated on the museum exhibition label,  1cIn keeping with the spontaneity of the design of such ceramics of Korea 19s past [Punch 19ong-ware], Choi Sungjae used his fingers to draw a picture of ducks swimming among reeds on this large vase. 1d  --  Gift of Bernard and Suzanne Pucker in honor of Stephen Kuman, RO46208^^Art Institute of Chicago; Chicago, IL^^Question:  Is this piece an example of what we, early 21st century American artists and students, would refer to as  1ccultural appropriation, 1d or is it something different from that? Commentary:  As I, the photographer/writer, looked at this piece, I was immediately struck by the energy of the piece, which was expressed for me through the boldness of the brush strokes in the slip, and the energy of the finger swipes through the slip.  I immediately  1cread 1d these as being strongly gestural elements in an essentially abstract statement, perhaps close, visually and conceptually, to, e.g., abstract expressionist painting, such as that of Franz Kline.  That, obviously, was a reading based entirely on the context that I brought to the work.  In fact, of course, there is nothing wrong with that and it may, in fact, attest to the validity of the work that it speaks across a cultural divide, evoking a strong aesthetic response.  Yet, of course, when I read the museum label, I learned that the finger drawn design was, in fact, a representational image, and one that is very meaningful when viewed in its own and proper context, the context of the Korean tradition of Punch 19ong-ware.  The initial reading of the spirit of energy of the work was accurate, and perhaps that was the most significant factor, but the reading of the specific image content was incorrect, out of context.  -- Extend this discussion through your own experiences of art.  What are your conclusions?^^Editorial assistant:  Joseph Augustin.  Collection librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
