  {
    "Title": "Atomic Bomb Dome",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Andrea",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Digital photograph.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Architecture; Cityscapes; Urban Scenes, WWII, World War II",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/2003",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "587 w x 785 h, 96 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "4061",
    "filename": [
      "4061-atomic-bomb-dome.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is the \"Genbaku Doomu\", or the Atomic Bomb Dome in Hiroshima. During the visit to the museum, I learned that it was predicted that nothing would grow in Hiroshima for 70 years. But it wasn`t long before grass and buds of flowers and trees began growing from the ashes of the burned city. Like a phoenix, Hiroshima was reborn, but not without a painful reminder of what once happened."
  },
