  {
    "Title": "Fan Painting of a Jesuit Church",
    "Creator": "Shoshu, Kano (1551-1601)",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf Colege",
    "Source": "Money Hickman et al, \"Japan's Golden Age: Momoyama\" Dallas Museum of Art, 1996, scan of image on p. 151,, pl. 42.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Painting; Christianity; Religious Art, Fine Art",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "863 w x 473 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "4057",
    "filename": [
      "4057-fan-painting-of-a-jesuit-church.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Although there is no consensus on which church is represented in this fan painting, most believe it to be the one on Shijobomon, due to its unusual three-story construction.  This painting was among a series of sixty-one fans painted by Kano Shoshu, mounted in an album showing famous sites in and around Kyoto, of which only twenty-four paintings are thought to survive. - abridged from catalogue entry by Christine Guth."
  },
