  {
    "Title": "Stone and Sand",
    "Creator": "Hagiwara Hideo (born 1913)",
    "Contributor": "Ross Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "2080 w x 1561 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "3855",
    "filename": [
      "3855-stone-and-sand.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Edition: 9/30. Woodblock print; ink, colors, and silver on paper. Hideo Hagiwara was born in Kofu City, Yamanishi Prefecture. Between 1921 and 1929 he lived in Korea and Manchuria. He studied at the Tokyo School of Fine Arts, where he graduated at the Oil Painting Section in 1938. While still there he attended Un'ichi Hiratsuka's extracurricular woodblock printing course, and in the same year he became quality controller at the Takamizawa Woodblock Print Company. He was conscripted into the army in 1943. In 1945 he had lost his house, his atelier and nearly all his early works. Around 1950 he had sufficiently recovered to start painting again. At the same time he started making Sosaku hanga (creative prints), both figurative and abstract subjects. He is known as a constant innovator and he is generally considered one of the best post-WWII Sosaku hanga artists.^^References: Helen Merritt and Nanako Yamada, Guide to Modern Japanese Prints: 1900-1975 Honolulu: University of Hawaii Press, 1992, p. 25. AND Frank L. Chance and Matthew Mizenko, Modern Impressions: Japanese Prints from the Berman and Corazza Collections. Collegeville, PA: Philip and Muriel Berman Museum of Art at Ursinus College, 2005, plate 32. For information on the artist visit: http://www.sosakuhanga.net/artists/hideo_hagiwara.html"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Pine Tree with Birds and Flowers",
    "Creator": "Anonymous artist of the Kano school",
    "Contributor": "Hallie Ford Museum of Art",
    "Institution": "Willamette University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "18th century, Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "3854",
    "filename": [
      "3854-pine-tree-with-birds-and-flowers.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Single six-panel screen. Ink, colors, and gold leaf on paper. 68\" x 132.5\". Although the artist is unidentified, there is a single jar-shaped seal, with the characters effaced, impressed on the right side. This shape seal is typically used by artists of the Kano school. This attribution concurs with the style of the painting, which is typical of Kano-school artists.    Normally screens are made in pairs, so this one is missing its mate. The presence of the seal on the right side, as well as the composition (with the pine tree on the right and body of water on the left) suggest this is the right side of a pair. One of the most interesting features of this painting is the appearance of black underdrawing outlines of flowers adjacent to the white flowers, composed of thickly applied pigment,  on the second panel from the right. These flowers were never meant to be visible in the finished painting. Their outlines were preliminary design features created by the artist in the process of sketching out his composition and then covered over by the gold leaf. Over time, the ink has bled through the gold which once obscured them.     Painting has undergone restoration twice, in the 1920s and again in 1974. ^^Gift of James and Aneta McIntyre. Purchased by the McIntyres from K. Yokoyama Co., Kyoto in 1957."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Portrait of Thoreau",
    "Creator": "Matsubara Naoko (born 1937)",
    "Contributor": "Ross Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints; Historical Figures",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "5052 w x 2442 h, 180 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "3853",
    "filename": [
      "3853-portrait-of-thoreau.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Edition: 45/100. Woodblock print; ink on paper.This is a fine, interesting work by this woman artist, indicative of modern Japanese artist-intellectuals' interest in Western philosophers.    Born in Tokushima, on the island of Shikoku, Matsubara Naoko grew up mostly in the city of Kyoto. Her father was one of the most senior Shinto priests in Japan, and her mother came from a very old Shinto family. After graduating from the Kyoto Academy of Fine Arts (now Kyoto Fine Arts University), she went to the United States as a Fulbright scholar, spending a year at the Carnegie Institute of Art (now Carnegie Mellon University) in Pittsburgh, where she received her MFA.^^References:Helen Merritt and Nanako Yamada, Guide to Modern Japanese Prints: 1900-1975. Honolulu: University of Hawaii Press, 1992, p. 86. AND Arlene Gehmacher, Klaas Ruitenbeek; introductory essay by John Rosenfield, Tree Spirit: the Woodcuts of Naoko Matsubara. Toronto: Royal Ontario Museum, 2003. For information on the artist visit: : http://naoko-matsubara.abbozzogallery.com/naoko-matsubara-biography.htm"
  },
