      "3830-rank-badge-part-of-set.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "These late nineteenth century rank badges were for use by holders of civil office (as opposed to military office).  Civil officials of the second rank were entitled to wear a badge depicting a golden pheasant; officials of the fifth rank used the emblem of a silver pheasant.  The bird emblems are surrounded by auspicious images.  These rank badges could be elaborately produced, utilizing a range of embroidery stitches, metallic thread, kesi tapestry weaving technique and appliqu??d motifs.  There are two golden pheasant rank badges in this set (although they have been photographed as one, apparently the photographer was unaware that there was a second identical badge below the top one); the one on the bottom is split up the center for attachment to the front of the garment.^^References: Valery M. Garrett, Mandarin Squares, Images of Asia (Hong Kong: Oxford University Press, 1990) AND Beverly Jackson and David Hugus, Ladder to the Clouds: Intrigue and Tradition in Chinese Rank (Berkeley: Ten Speed Press, 1999)"
  },
