  {
    "Title": "Page from the book Toshisen ehon gogon zekku - Illustrated selection of poems of the Tang dynasty, poems of four verses, each verse of five words",
    "Creator": "Illustrated by Tachibana Sekiho (active late 18th century)",
    "Contributor": "Ross Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "2156 w x 1540 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "3829",
    "filename": [
      "3829-page-from-the-book-toshisen-ehon-gogon-zekku-illus.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Double page woodblock printed book illustration; ink on paper. (The museum owns two additional pages from this book). Because the page has been separated from its book, there is no way to know which edition it came from. This is a good example that demonstrates the widespread and popular interest in ancient Chinese literature among sophisticated, well educated commoners (the readers of books such as these) in the Edo period. There exist several printed books with close variations on this title, including one illustrated by Hokusai. But this is clearly not the Hokusai volume.^^Reference: C.H. Mitchell, The Illustrated Books of the Nanga, Maruyama, Shijo and Other Related Schools of Japan, A Biobibliography. Los Angeles: Dawson's Book Shop, 1972, pp. 535-536."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Pair of ancestor portraits (male)",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Wittenberg University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "20th century, Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1536 w x 2282 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "3828",
    "filename": [
      "3828-pair-of-ancestor-portraits-male.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Dating from the late 19th or early 20th century, these two ancestor paintings are typical of the genre.  The individuals are in formal poses and each wears formal clothing.  He wears formal court dress; she is shown in wedding finery, including an elaborate crown and a red robe.  Both have rank badges on the front of their garments.  Women were denied any role in officialdom, but were entitled to wear the rank badge of their husband.  In the late nineteenth century it was customary for the portrait of the wife to be placed at the right side of that of her husband (on the viewer???s left).  The use of shading on the faces indicates the influence of Western art and most likely that of photography.  The cranes depicted at the bottom of the portraits are symbols of longevity.^^References: Jan Stuart and Evelyn Rawski, Worshipping the Ancestors:  Chinese Commemorative Portraits (Washington, DC: Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery, 2001)."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Page from an Unidentified Woodblock Book",
    "Creator": "Illustration in the style of Katsushika Hokusai (1760-1849)",
    "Contributor": "Ross Art Museum",
