    "Creator": "Unidentified artist, possibly Utagawa Kunisada (1786-1864)",
    "Contributor": "Ross Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints; Theater",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "19th century, Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1367 w x 2084 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "3816",
    "filename": [
      "3816-kabuki-actor-at-his-dressing-table.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Woodblock print, surimono type, ink, colors, and embossing on paper. Surimono were limited edition, fine quality prints produced for a small, select group of clients, in this case, fans of the actor portrayed.^^Reference: Roger S. Keyes, \"Surimono,\" The Hotei Encyclopedia of Japanese Woodblock Prints edited by Amy Reigle Newland. Amsterdam: Hotei Publishing, 2005, pp. 221-224."
  },
