    "filename": [
      "3732-zar-zari-zar-baksh-dargah-ritual-of-thanksgiving-f.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Family members wait to perform the ritual thanking the saint for helping them to conceive a healthy child."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Zar Zari Zar Baksh Dargah, ritual of thanksgiving for child",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Benton, Cathy",
    "Institution": "Lake Forest College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Copyright: April 2003 Cathy Benton. Images and content on this site are available for educational and research purposes, provided due recognition is given to the author. Transmission or reproduction of protected items beyond fair use requires the written permission of the photographer.",
    "Subject": "Islam; Ritual; Sites; Children",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "India",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/2003",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1280 w x 960 h",
    "Collection": "Religious Sites in Maharashtra",
    "id": "3731",
    "filename": [
      "3731-zar-zari-zar-baksh-dargah-ritual-of-thanksgiving-f.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "At this shrine, couples pray to the saint, Zar Zari Zar Baksh, for his help in conceiving a healthy child.   When the child is old enough, the couples promise tol return and make an offering of thanksgiving.  This ritual consists of distributing sweetbreads equal in weight to that of the child.  To determine this weight, two metal crates are balanced by a rope hanging over the limb of a large tree in the courtyard of the dargah.  Often travelling from great distances, families dress in their finest clothes and bring many family members to share in this festive celebratory ritual."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Bibi ki Maqbara, entrance sign",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Benton, Cathy",
    "Institution": "Lake Forest College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Copyright: April 2003 Cathy Benton. Images and content on this site are available for educational and research purposes, provided due recognition is given to the author. Transmission or reproduction of protected items beyond fair use requires the written permission of the photographer.",
    "Subject": "Islam; Sites; Ritual; Social Customs",
    "Language": "English, Hindi",
    "Country": "India",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/2003",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1280 w x 960 h",
    "Collection": "Religious Sites in Maharashtra",
    "id": "3730",
    "filename": [
      "3730-bibi-ki-maqbara-entrance-sign.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "At the entrance to the shrine, visitors are instructed to remove their shoes and sandals (chapples).  The sign in English and Hindi indicates that while you are expected to remove your footwear at this shrine, the shrine takes no responsibility for their care.  In other words, perhaps you might want to pay the man at the entrance to watch them for you.  It's interesting that the sign is only in English and Hindi, not in Urdu or Marathi."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Bibi ki Maqbara, marble carving detail",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Benton, Cathy",
    "Institution": "Lake Forest College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Copyright: April 2003 Cathy Benton. Images and content on this site are available for educational and research purposes, provided due recognition is given to the author. Transmission or reproduction of protected items beyond fair use requires the written permission of the photographer.",
