    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1200 w  x  763 h, 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3663",
    "filename": [
      "3663-kinkakuji-garden-pond-detail.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Reflection of the first floor of the Golden Pavilion in the garden pond.  The Golden Pavilion was built by Yoshimitsu, 3rd Ashikaga shogun, in the late 1300s, when he retired from the office of shogun.  The pond was part of an earlier, Heian period estate.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ginkakuji, sand mound in main garden",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "Image taken at site by M. Thiedeman",
    "Rights": "May be used only for educational, non-commercial purposes, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project and to the photographer. Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Gardens",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1200 w  x  798 h, 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3662",
    "filename": [
      "3662-ginkakuji-sand-mound-in-main-garden.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The cone or mound of sand is just across a segment of the pond, perhaps 60 feet to the north-north east of the Silver Pavilion.  Sometimes referred to as the \"moon reflecting mound,\" along with the adjacent \"silver sand beach,\" it is said to have been intended to reflect moon light into the Silver Pavilion, or to have provided a point for the contemplation of the moon light and shadows.  It is a comparatively recent addition to the garden design at Ginkakuji, having been added during the Edo period. In this view of the mound, looking directly east, there is a rich play between the sand mound and the rock and schrub in the foreground.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ginkakuji, garden walk",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "Image taken at site by M. Thiedeman",
    "Rights": "May be used only for educational, non-commercial purposes, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project and to the photographer. Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Gardens",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "798 w  x  1200 h, 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3661",
    "filename": [
      "3661-ginkakuji-garden-walk.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Ginkakuji (Jishoji), Kyoto.  Walk in garden near entrance, outside wall surrounding garden proper.  The geometry is a striking contrast to the main garden. The Ginkakuji compound built by Ashikaga Yoshimasa, c. 1479.  Date of this walk same or later?  [The light reflecting mound of the main garden was an Edo period addition.]^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
