    "id": "3603",
    "filename": [
      "3603-hiroshima-medical-care-at-a-relief-station.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The First Elementary School, 2,600 meters from the hypocenter, and other building that survived the blast throughout the city were used as relief stations to provide the very minimal aid that was available to the victims of the blast.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Burn victims of the blast, 01.",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Earlham College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima",
    "Rights": "May be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project, Earlham College, and the Peace Memorial Museum of Hiroshima.",
    "Subject": "-Hiroshima; Atomic bomb victims",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1500 w.  x  777 h., 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3602",
    "filename": [
      "3602-hiroshima-burn-victims-of-the-blast-01.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The first wave of the horrendous toll taken by the atomic bomb explosion was, of course, the concussion of the explosion and unearthly intensity of the heat of the explosion.  These immediate effects were followed months and years later by illnesses resulting from exposure to radioactivity, such as the death of Sadako Sasaki, who succumbed to leukemia ten years later. In this image we see the unimaginable horror of the burns resulting from the blast, which generated heat rays so intense that they charred the patterns of fabrics on to victims' skin.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Watch and buckle of a victim.",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michaal",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima",
    "Rights": "May be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project, Earlham College, and the Peace Memorial Museum of Hiroshima.",
    "Subject": "-Hiroshima; Atomic bomb victims",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "735 w.  x  948 h., 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3601",
    "filename": [
      "3601-hiroshima-watch-and-buckle-of-a-victim.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This pocket watch and belt buckle belonged to Jiro Hataguchi, who was at work, at the Hiroshima Railway Bureau, at the time of the blast.  His wife and brother found the watch and buckle and bones four days after the exposion under a safe in his workplace, 1850 meters from the hypocenter.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Survivors immediately after the blast.",
    "Creator": "Matsushige, Yoshito",
    "Contributor": "Earlham College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima",
    "Rights": "May be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project, Earlham College, and the Peace Memorial Museum of Hiroshima.",
    "Subject": "-Hiroshima; Atomic bomb victims",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1226 w.  x  1125 h., 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3600",
    "filename": [
      "3600-hiroshima-survivors-immediately-after-the-blast.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Photographs of the immediate after-effects of the A-bomb are very rare.  This photo was one of perhaps half a dozen or fewer taken by resident Yoshito Matsushige.  It was taken at about 11:00 a.m., on the morning of August 6, at the west end of the Miyuki Bridge, Senda-machi, about 2,270 meters from the hypocenter.  It shows survivors of the blast seeking aid for burns and other injuries.  The photo has been enlarged to a mural sized image in the Peace Memorial Museum.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Sadako Sasaki",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Earlham College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima",
    "Rights": "May be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project, Earlham College, and the Peace Memorial Museum of Hiroshima.",
    "Subject": "-Hiroshima; Atomic bomb victims",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "906 w.  x  975 h., 150ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3599",
    "filename": [
      "3599-hiroshima-sadako-sasaki.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "A sobering image of the young girl, Sadako Sasaki, in her coffin at her funeral on October 26, 1955.  Although she had appeared to have escaped harm from the A-bomb blast at the age of two, she succumbed to leukemia ten years later.  This image is in the Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima, courtesy of Shigeo and Masahiro Sasaki.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Sadako Sasaki's Paper Cranes.",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima",
    "Rights": "May be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project, Earlham College, and the Peace Memorial Museum of Hiroshima.",
    "Subject": "-Hiroshima; Atomic bomb victims",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1402 w.  x  1125 h., 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3598",
    "filename": [
      "3598-hiroshima-sadako-sasakis-paper-cranes.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "These origami paper cranes were among those distributed at her funeral.  In a story now known worldwide by millions of school children, Sadako Sasaki was exposed to the atomic bomb as an infant of two years age.  She appeared to have escaped harm from the exposure, until ten years later, when, in her sixth year in elementary school, she suddenly became ill with leukemia.  She was hospitalized and fought for her life for eight months, before succumbing to the leukemia.  During her illness, she continually folded paper cranes, believing that they would help her to recover, and the paper cranes have come to be a symbol of both tragedy and hope.  Sadako's death gave birth to a movement to erect a monument in the Peace Park to all of the children who perished in the A-bomb explosion.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Black Rain.",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima",
    "Rights": "May be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to IDEAS project, Earlham College, and the Peace Memorial Museum of Hiroshima.",
    "Subject": "-Hiroshima; -blast effect",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1125 w.  x  1006 h., 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3597",
    "filename": [
      "3597-hiroshima-black-rain.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is a section of a white wall from a house that was 3,700 meters from the hypocenter.  The roof of the house had been set askew by the force of the atomic blast, allowing the black rain that fell following the blast to run down the white plaster wall, staining it.  Analysis of the stains indicated that the black rain contained radioactive fallout from the atomic bomb blast.  This section of wall was donated by Akijiro Yashima, and it is now displayed in the Peace Memorial Museum, Hiroshima.^^Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send comments or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Department of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA. email address: thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hiroshima:  Victims of the atomic bomb blast.",
    "Creator": "",
