    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "960 w  x  720 h, 96 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3423",
    "filename": [
      "3423-printed-fortunes-and-prayers-omikuji-at-shinto-shr.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "These are folded pieces of paper with printed fortunes or prayers on them, obtained at the local shrine.  They are tied here and left at the Shinto shrine in the hope that the kami of the shrine will help to make the fortune come true or help to fulfill the prayer.^^Editorial assistant:  Sandy Augustin.  Collection Librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Shinto shrine, Shichigosan Day, children are brought to the local shrine",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Celebrations; Children; Shinto; Ritual",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "794 w  x  720 h,  96 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3422",
    "filename": [
      "3422-shinto-shrine-shichigosan-day-children-are-brought.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The children here are arriving at a shrine in late October for the celebration of Shichigosan  --  Seven - five - three Day.  On this day, girls who are seven or three years old and boys who are five are brought to their shrine in their best dress or in traditional dress for prayers for their well being, for a blessing.   --   This particular Shichigosan celebration was on October 28, 2000, and was at the Hachiman Shrine in Morioka.  The Hachiman Shrine is the primary Shinto shrine of Morioka, which is in Iwate Prefecture, on the Pacific side of northern most Honshu.^^Editorial assistant:  Sandy Augustin.  Collection Librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Shinto shrine, an infant is brought to the local shrine for a birth ritual",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Celebrations; Family; Shinto; Ritual; Children",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "960 w  x  720 h, 96 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3421",
    "filename": [
      "3421-shinto-shrine-an-infant-is-brought-to-the-local-sh.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "When an infant is one month old, it is taken by its parents to the local shrine for miyamairi, a birth ritual.  By this ritual, the infant becomes a member of the shrine and is placing under the protection of the kami, the guardian spirit of the shrine.  Traditionally, this is an infant's first trip out of its home.^^Editorial assistant:  Sandy Augustin.  Collection Librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Store interior, a new \"super-store\"",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Stores; Social Change",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "960 w  x  615 h, 96 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3420",
    "filename": [
      "3420-store-interior-a-new-super-store.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is a view of the interior of a new store in Japan.  It is the type of store that would be referred to as a \"super store\" or \"super center\" in the U.S.  I.e., it carries groceries, drug store items and sundries, stationary, books and magazines, household items, etc.  The emergence of this style of retailing is relatively recent in Japan.^^The emergence of the \"super store\" retailing model in Japan reflects the common ownership of automobiles (or access to company owned vehicles), which became common in Japan only in the last quarter of the 20th century.  The shift from neighborhood-based, family-owned and run businesses to this corporate structure may have what consequences for the structure of community in Japan?^^Editorial assistant:  Sandy Augustin.  Collection Librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "School -- students'  kendo sticks from an after school club",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.   Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Education; Martial Arts",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "960 w  x  635 h, 96 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3419",
    "filename": [
      "3419-school-students-kendo-sticks-from-an-after-school.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "All middle school students are required to participate in after -school activity clubs at the school.  They are free to select which clubs they wish to join, but participation is mandatory.  The clubs, of course, are group activities, an important part of education in Japan.  Many of the clubs focus on areas of traditional Japanese culture, such as tea ceremony or ikebana.  This photo shows the wooden swords - kendo sticks - of students belonging to the club that learns and practices the traditional art of kendo.^^Editorial assistant:  Sandy Augustin.  Collection Librarian:  Amy Bryant.  Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
