    "id": "3323",
    "filename": [
      "3323-korean-ceramics-bottle-buncheong-ware.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Bottle, stoneware.  The form was covered with a white slip applied with a brush.  The design on the piece was created by using a sharp tool to cut through the white slip coating, revealing the darker clay underneath, in the manner used in Buncheong ware.  The Avery Brundage Collection, B67P41^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics: Jar, Buncheong ware.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Choson dynasty",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1039 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3325",
    "filename": [
      "3325-korean-ceramics-jar-buncheong-ware.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "A modest sized Buncheong ware jar, perhaps 5 inches in height. Stoneware with a white slip that was applied thickly with a coarse brush that left a sense of the gesture of the brush stroke on the surface.  The form of this piece calls to mind the similar forms of pieces made in Japan in the 16th century, on the island of Kyushu, by Korean potters, such as those who created Takatori-ware in present-day Fukuoka Prefecture.  An example of such a piece may be seen in image soc000146, in the St. Olaf College collection, Asian Take Out. Gift of Mr. Arthur J. McTaggart, 1998.25^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Look carefully at Korean pottery and then at the Takatori and Karatsu wares produced in Japan. Identify the elements of Korean influence in the Japanese work.  What elements of Korean ceramics appealed particularly to Japanese tea masters?^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics:  Bottle, Buncheong ware.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Choson dynasty",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "761 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3324",
    "filename": [
      "3324-korean-ceramics-bottle-buncheong-ware.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Bottle, stoneware.  The form was covered with a contrasting white slip. Several horizontal bands were created by incising horizontal lines through the white slip.  Within the horizontal bands, areas, defined by those incised lines, the potter or decorator then used brush and iron oxide to paint design motives on the surface, with the glaze then being applied over the decoration.  The Avery Brundage Collection, B65P63^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics: Bottle, Buncheong ware.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Choson dynasty",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "734 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3326",
    "filename": [
      "3326-korean-ceramics-bottle-buncheong-ware.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Bottle, stoneware.  In this example, the decorative technique employed was quite different from the other two Bundheong ware bottles shown here.  In this instance, the surface of the piece was stamped with a pattern, perhaps made of fired clay.  The surface of the piece was then coated with white slip (porcelain), including the impressed pattern elements.  The surface was then scraped clean of the white slip, revealing the darker underlying clay, while the white clay remained in the  indentations stamped into the surface, creating the contrasting pattern of light and dark that we see here. The Avery Brundage Collection, B60P388^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^The three stoneware bottles shown here are presented in one case in the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.  This one, ecasia000386, is dated 1400 - 1500, while the other two, ecasia000384 and 000385, are dated 1500-1590. With that in mind, study the forms of all three pieces very carefully, noting especially details such as the relative thickness of the rims, the degree of convexity of the necks, and the height of the foot rings of the pieces.  Research other examples of similar pieces in books and ask whether these differences are accidental or whether they represent subtle changes in a tradition over time.  Study carefully also the proportions created on the surfaces of all three pieces by the banding that is employed in the decoration.  Do you find similar proportional relationships in other Korean pieces of the same period or from other periods; is this sense of surface division a constant in Korean art or peculiar to these bottles? How do the dividing points relate to the forms of the pieces?^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics: Jar in the shape of a barrel, porcelain.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Choson dynasty",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1304 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3322",
    "filename": [
      "3322-korean-ceramics-jar-in-the-shape-of-a-barrel-porce.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This piece presents an interesting contrast to the elegance of most of the other Korean forms presented here, images ecasia000382 through ecasia000387.  This piece is heavy and broadly proportioned when compared with the other bottle forms with their narrow, soaring necks.  Technically, it is an interesting piece, having been constructed obviously of three separate pieces.  The body of the piece appears to have been formed by throwing two bowl forms which were then joined together, rim to rim, in the same manner as appeared to have been the case with the \"Moon Jar\" shown in image ecasia000358.  The neck of this jar is also a separately thrown piece, a cylindrical form which was joined to the top of the piece to form its neck. The Avery Brundage Collection, B60P926^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Research this piece in books on Korean pottery to see if you can identify what the function of the piece would have been and who might have used such a piece.  Does that information account for the difference in form from the pieces shown in images ecasia000384, 000385, and 000386?^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
