  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics: Jar with dragon decoration, porcelain.",
    "Creator": "unknown",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Choson dynasty",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1423 w  x  1114 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3320",
    "filename": [
      "3320-korean-ceramics-jar-with-dragon-decoration-porcela.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "A fairly large porcelain piece, very full in form.  The surface of the piece functions as a canvas for the very bold, energetic brush decoration that covers the entire surface.  The image is that of a dragon, a frequent theme in east Asian art, twisting as it moves through the air between clouds.  The brush decoration is iron oxide applied under the glaze.^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics: Vase with reeds and egret, buncheong style ware.",
    "Creator": "Park Young-sook (1947 -   )",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "871 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3319",
    "filename": [
      "3319-korean-ceramics-vase-with-reeds-and-egret-buncheon.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Vase with reeds and egret, 1998, is a modern piece by Park Young-sook (1947 -   ).  The simple stoneware form was covered with white slip, which was then drawn through with a sharp tool, revealing the darker clay body beneath.  Bits of clay and copper-oxide were added to form the tops of the reeds and the entire piece was then covered with a clear glaze contained a small amount of iron oxide, which produces the light green color, known as celadon.  The techniques employed by the artist call deliberately on earlier traditions of  Korean pottery, the buncheong tradition of the Joseon (Yi) dynasty (1392-1910). Gift of Dr. and Mrs. Marvin Gordon, 1999.48^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Korean ceramics: Dish with Pine Tree, Buncheong-style ware.",
    "Creator": "Min Young-ki (1947 -  ), potter and Chun Sung-woo (1934 -   ), painter",
    "Contributor": "Thiedeman, Michael",
    "Institution": "Earlham College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only, in accordance with Fair Use policies.  Acknowledgement to be given to the IDEAS Project and to Earlham College and to the Asian Art Museum of San Francisco.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Pottery, Korean",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Korea",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1241 w  x  1125 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Cultures Collection",
    "id": "3318",
    "filename": [
      "3318-korean-ceramics-dish-with-pine-tree-buncheong-styl.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This large dish is a collaborative piece, created by Min Young-ki, potter, and Chun Sung-woo, painter, both famous contemporary artists.  The piece is in the style of Buncheong-ware.  In this piece, the dish was created and coated with a white slip (clay in a liquid state); the image of a tall pine tree was painted on top of the white slip, and the piece was then covered with glaze and fired.  Gift of Chung Yang-mo, 2004.14^^Asian Art Museum of San Francisco, CA^^Editorial assistant: Joseph Augustin; Collection Librarian: Amy Bryant. Please send any questions, comments, or content corrections to Michael Thiedeman, Professor of Art, Earlham College, Richmond, IN 47374-4095 USA.  email address:  thiedmi@earlham.edu"
  },
