    "Subject": "Business; People; Government; Politicians; Historical Figures",
    "Language": "English",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/1972",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "573 w x 768 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3224",
    "filename": [
      "3224-nixon-in-china.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "In this cover photo from Newsweek, President Richard Nixon toasts Chinese Premier Zhou Enlai in the Great Hall of the People in March, 1972.^^China had rejoined the United Nations in 1971, displacing Taiwan.  Nixon's visit produced the Shanghai Communique in which the U.S. acknowledged the 'one China' doctrine.  Congress subsequently passed the \"\"Taiwan Relations Act\"\" which provided for continuing relations with Taiwan. It is argued that by playing the China card, Nixon intended to restrain the Soviet threat."
  },
