  {
    "Title": "Resplendent Light on the Evening of National Day",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lepore, Jamie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Manhua Sept. 23 1958 no. 121",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "National Identity; Print; Celebrations",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/1958",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "617 w x 768 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3105",
    "filename": [
      "3105-resplendent-light-on-the-evening-of-national-day.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Front cover illustration: Beacons fill the sky over Tiananmen Square, serving as arrows indicating \"The Great Leap Forward\" in the production of grain, steel, and machinery.  Large bins of grain from a bumper harvest are seen to the left, while new factory chimneys rise on the right.^^Unfortunately, the Great Leap Forward was a failure and the principle cause of massive starvation and malnutrition in 1959-61."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Dogmatism",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lepore, Jamie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Manhua May 23 no. 89 scan of image on cover.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Print; Painting; Social Change; Politicians; Arts, Fine Art, Business and Politics, Contemporary Leaders, History, Historical Events,Mao, social commentary, campaigns, political cartoons",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "596 w x 768 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3104",
    "filename": [
      "3104-dogmatism.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "An official in this illustration is depicted carrying an umbrella labelled \"dogmatism,\" refusing to let the rain water the flowers and plants.  A writer nearby asks him \"Why won't you let the flowers and sprouts get some spring rain?\" The official answers \"That would never do!  As soon as there is rain, poisonous weeds would grow up.\"^^In 1957, Chairman Mao advocated free expression, or \"letting one hundred flowers bloom,\" in order to provide the party with useful criticism.  Shotly therafter, those who spoke up were denounced during the subsequent \"anti-rightist\" campaign.  \"Poisonous weeds\" are critics who need to be weeded out."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Dragon Surrenders",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lepore, Jamie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Manhua no. 76 scan of image on cover.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Print; Government; National Identity",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "582 w x 768 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3103",
    "filename": [
      "3103-dragon-surrenders.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Illustration from Manhua showing Soviet engineers helping to tame the dragon of the Heilongjiang, or 'Black Dragon River,' in China's far northeast.^^Within four years, the Chinese and Soviets had come to loggerheads over the issue of signing the nuclear test-ban treaty, and Soviet experts all departed China.  In the late sixties, the Sino-Soviet border in Heilongjiang province became the scene of armed clashes between the two countries."
  },
  {
    "Title": "One Person Blows the Flute, While the Other Plays",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lepore, Jamie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Manhua, July 8 1959, no. 140 scan of cover image.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Military; Gender Roles; Poster; Japanese occupation of China",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "01/01/1959",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "595 w x 768 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3102",
    "filename": [
      "3102-one-person-blows-the-flute-while-the-other-plays.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Illustration from Manhua showing an American military officer in the picture playing a tune on the 'flute' (actually an American missile) while the Japanese geisha provides the breath.  She carries a fan labelled \"Revising the Security Treaty.\"^^Having fought an eight-year war of resistance against Japan (1937-1945), China was wary of a revival of Japanese militarism and of the U.S.-Japan alliance.  Chinese continue to worry about the U.S.-Japan alliance and continue to seek reparations for Japanese war crimes."
  },
