    "Contributor": "Lepore, Jamie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "The Nineties (jiushi niandai) 1997 vol. 3 scan of image on cover",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Print; Politicians; National Identity",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "549 w x 768 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3080",
    "filename": [
      "3080-farewell-deng-xiaoping.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Cover illustration from the Hong Kong newsmagazine \"The Nineties\" showing Deng Xiaoping playing bridge.  Deng died just a few months before Hong Kong's return to China.^^When Deng Xiaoping was restored to power in 1978, many Chinese believed he would bring China back on the right track, bringing peace and prosperity, and put up signs saying \"\"Hello, Xiaoping.\"\"  Those feelings largely changed after the crackdown on the 1989 student movement."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Emperor Go-Mizunoo",
    "Creator": "Gen'yo Shonin [1634-1727]",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Yoshiaki Shimizu, ed., \"Japan: The Shaping of Daimyo Culture 1185-1868\" Washington: National Gallery of Art, 1988, pl. 19.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Painting; Calligraphy; Historical Figures",
    "Language": "Japanese",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "424 w x 768 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3079",
    "filename": [
      "3079-emperor-go-mizunoo.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Ink and color painted I\\image of the Tokugawa-era emperor Go-Mizunoo. The two poems were copied from inscriptions on other portraits of the emperor. The translation by Watanabe Akiyoshi is as follows: \"Painful, this/withered tree fence hidden/ in the deep mountain;/ would that at least my heart's/ flowers were fragrantly abloom./ My life being thus,/ in this world that I will never revisit/ the thought of leaving a trace/ of my calligraphy for a moment-/ even that is sad.\"   The artist Gen'yo, a Zen Buddhist nun, was Go-Mizunoo's granddaughter."
  },
