  {
    "Title": "Tale of Genji Designs on Set of Shelves",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Money Hickman et al, \"Japan's Golden Age: Momoyama\" Dallas Museum of Art, 1996, scan of image on p. 249, pl. 119.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Decorative Arts; Literature; Classical literature",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "723 w x 731 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3066",
    "filename": [
      "3066-tale-of-genji-designs-on-set-of-shelves.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This three-tier set of zushidana-type shelves includes a cabinet on the middle level in which the doors swing out and another lower level with a sliding door.  The decorative motifs are based on the Heian-period romantic classic, The Tale of Genji.  In addition to the lacquer and pulverized stone used in the motif, inlaid mother-of-pearl, gold, silver, and tin are also employed."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Bowl",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Yoshiaki Shimizu, ed., \"Japan: The Shaping of Daimyo Culture 1185-1868\" Washington: National Gallery of Art, 1988, pl. 245.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Ceramics; Food",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "855 w x 788 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "2494",
    "filename": [
      "2494-bowl.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "From Phan, Thailand, stoneware. H: 3\" x Dia: 6\". The kilns of Phan were discovered in 1962, and, like that of Sankampaeng, they produced ceramics for domestic use, rather than export like the Sawankhalok and Sukhothai kilns. The ceramics from these kilns seem to be almost exclusively celadons. The clay is a light color and often the glazes are very fine. This small, deep-sided bowl is heavily potted with a pale body and fine colored glaze."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Writing Table and Writing Utensil Box",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Yoshiaki Shimizu, ed., \"Japan: The Shaping of Daimyo Culture 1185-1868\" Washington: National Gallery of Art, 1988, pl. 224.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Decorative Arts; Aesthetics; Leisure Activities",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1573-1615",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "481 w x 768 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "3064",
    "filename": [
      "3064-writing-table-and-writing-utensil-box.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Bundai (writing table) and suzuribako (writing utensil box) decorated with a combination of bamboo, paulownia, and the phoenix.  The background is done using a technique known as nashiji, similar in appearance to the skin of the nashi, or Japanese pear, in which metal flakes are suspended in lacquer."
  },
