  {
    "Title": "Fan painting - Calligraphy on round fan",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "Collection of Groke Mickey",
    "Institution": "Washington and Lee University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Calligraphy; Painting",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2866",
    "filename": [
      "2866-fan-painting-calligraphy-on-round-fan.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Eight lines of running script; two lines with the date and dedication. The writer has not been identified. The date could be 60 years later.    The round format is not that common in the collection. Round or slightly oval fans are a much older form of fan, and examples with the calligraphy of Song Emperors survive in modern collections. The writing, in a fluid running script, is also suggestive of that era."
  },
