  {
    "Title": "Fan painting - Orchids, rocks and mushrooms - detail of inscription",
    "Creator": "Shen Rong (act. 1820-1850)",
    "Contributor": "Collection of Groke Mickey",
    "Institution": "Watson Pavilion, Washington and Lee University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Calligraphy; Painting",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2829",
    "filename": [
      "2829-fan-painting-orchids-rocks-and-mushrooms-detail-of.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "To the right are two five character quatrains comparing the orchid to the fragrance of a woman. At the end is the dedication and signature. Shen Rong, zi Shixiang, has a minimal presence in the literature.  He was known for his flowers, as well as landscape in the manner of the Loudong School, associated with the early Qing master Wang Yuanqi. The single work cited in Sir??n is also of an orchid, and Sir??n states that he was active around 1830.     To the right are two five-character quatrains comparing the orchid to the fragrance of a woman. At the end is the dedication and signature. The literary and pictorial conceit of the wild orchid is a very old one in China, and one that a student could follow in an essay on the topic. The orchid is unobtrusive, not at all showy like many seen in greenhouses today, yet its fragrance pervades the air. This is a metaphor for the proper Confucian gentleman, whose character influences others although he may be retired socially. The long pliant leaves of the plant allow the calligraphic possibilities of the brush to come into play, and the solidity of the rock contrasts with the softness of the plant. The mushroom is always a symbol of longevity, sometimes associated with Daoist practices."
  },
