  {
    "Title": "Bronze ding vessel",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Wittenberg University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1712 w x 1912 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2768",
    "filename": [
      "2768-bronze-ding-vessel.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is a standard example of the most popular and enduring bronze vessel shapes.  It is an excellent example of mould casting.  Normally, the design on the lid of an ancient bronze vessel matches that on the body of a vessel.  Here, the decoration on the lid does not repeat that of the body; nor does the lid fit securely on the vessel.  These two discrepancies indicate that this lid does not belong to this specific vessel.  Bronze lids with similar three projecting prongs have been found in tombs in Sandong and Henan Provinces; the vessels they belong to are considered to date from the Eastern Zhou period (722-256 BC).  The surface decoration of interlaced designs both the body and the lid are typical for this period."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Rubbing of stone engraving depiction of the poetess Xie Tao (detail)",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Wittenberg University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1392 w x 1292 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2767",
    "filename": [
      "2767-rubbing-of-stone-engraving-depiction-of-the-poetes.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Although of lesser quality, this depiction of Xie Tao is interesting because it is a rare (imaginary) portrayal  of a woman writer.  The text at the top of the scroll is her biography.  Xie (768 - 831/32) was a noted courtesan/poetess who lived in Chengdu, Sichuan.  In addition to her poetry she is famous for developing an ornamented paper to be used for writing out brief poems.^^Reference: Brocade River Poems: Selected Works of the Tang Dynasty Courtesan Xie Tao.  Tr. and intro. by Jeanne Larsen (Princeton: Princeton University Press, 1987)."
  },
