  },
  {
    "Title": "Untitled",
    "Creator": "Qi Baishi (1863-1957)",
    "Contributor": "Flaten Art Museum",
    "Institution": "St Olaf College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1216",
    "filename": [
      "1216-untitled.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Woodcut on fabric, 15 1/8 x 11 inches. Very little information is available about this artist. This work shows a white horse, drawn in outline, with the background tinted and the animal's white color, the color of the untinted paper. This manner of depicting horses is well known in ink painting in Japan.  Ink painter Shukei Sesson (1504-89), for instance, did a very famous example, known as the White horses (hakubazu), a work now in the collection of Shikajima Shrine.  Wakyosai's horses look particularly like Sesson's in how they use line. Consider the line that defines the chest of Wakyosai's horse and the top of its right foreleg.  The line has the \"calligraphic\" or thick and thin character of a brushstroke.  It begins thick, turns right, curves downwards, and then ends.  It starts up again thin, curves left, thickens once more and, reversing itself, ends in a series of closely spaced parallel lines. Not only does Wakyosai's line go thick and thin like a brushstroke, but also the parallel lines at its bottom reproduce the effect of the brush running out of ink.  Similarly, the turn at the beginning of the line at the top of the chest suggests how the \"belly\" of the brush touched down as the brushstroke was made. The shape of this line to the right and the left at this point are noticeably different.  The left side is shaped like a 'c', whereas the right side forms an 'S' curve.  The difference in the two sides of the line means that it can suggest, on the one hand, how the muscle inside the horse's chest bulges out and, on the other, how the skin outside is continuously smooth."
  },
  {
