  {
    "Title": "Two Thai pots (pot 2)",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Sociology-Anthropology Department",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Thailand",
    "Region": "",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1347 w x 1537 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2618",
    "filename": [
      "2618-two-thai-pots-pot-2.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Allegedly from the ancient Thailand site of Ban Chiang. Painted earthenware. When this archaeological site was discovered in the 1960s, it predated the earliest known bronze age site in Thailand, and southeast Asian prehistory was rewritten. Ban Chiang was occupied for over 2000 years prior to the Common Era and its accidental discovery pushed the date of civilization in southeast Asia back nearly two millennia. Ban Chiang is a UNESCO World Heritage Area site, considered the most important prehistoric settlement so far discovered in South-East Asia. It marks an important stage in human cultural, social and technological evolution in the prehistoric era of Southeast Asia, 3600 BCE-200 CE.^^The department acquired these in 1992 from a generous gift by Mr. Charles E. McManis. Information from James Peoples, (2) Two pots, two rollers (or seals?), a bronze bracelet and fragments of an apparent bronze necklace from ancient Thailand, allegedly from the Ban Chiang archaeological site."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Two Thai pots (pot 1)",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Sociology-Anthropology Department",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Thailand",
    "Region": "",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1428 w x 1428 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2617",
    "filename": [
      "2617-two-thai-pots-pot-1.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Allegedly from the ancient Thailand site of Ban Chiang. Painted earthenware. When this archaeological site was discovered in the 1960s, it predated the earliest known bronze age site in Thailand, and southeast Asian prehistory was rewritten. Ban Chiang was occupied for over 2000 years prior to the Common Era and its accidental discovery pushed the date of civilization in southeast Asia back nearly two millennia. Ban Chiang is a UNESCO World Heritage Area site, considered the most important prehistoric settlement so far discovered in South-East Asia. It marks an important stage in human cultural, social and technological evolution during the prehistoric era in Southeast Asia 3600 BCE - 200 CE.^^The department acquired these in 1992 from a generous gift by Mr. Charles E. McManis. Information from James Peoples, OWU: The collection includes two main categories of objects:  (1) 137 coins, 46 amulets, and 2 chatelaines from Korea. (2) Two pots, two rollers (or seals?), a bronze bracelet and fragments of an apparent bronze necklace from ancient Thailand, allegedly from the Ban Chiang archaeological site. Thai objects are from the prehistoric bronze age village of Ban Chiang."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ladder",
    "Creator": "Kim Lim (1936-1997)",
    "Contributor": "Ross Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Singapore",
    "Region": "",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1623 w x 1611 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2616",
    "filename": [
      "2616-ladder.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Intaglio print on paper. British sculptor and printmaker of Chinese birth. She grew up in Singapore and at the age of 18 decided to go to London to study at Saint Martin's School of Art (1954-6) where she took a particular interest in wood-carving; she then transferred to the Slade School of Art, where she concentrated on printmaking, graduating in 1960. Whilst at college she often traveled through Asia and Europe en route back to Singapore, with Indian and South-East Asian sculpture and spirituality making a great impact on her work. While Lim always acknowledged a debt to the work of Constantin Brancusi in her simplification and abstraction of forms, it is in her concern for the specific qualities of materials, as in her use of charred wood to create contrast, that the influence of Eastern spirituality and concepts of balance can be seen. In 1960 she married the painter and sculptor William Turnbull, settling in London but continuing to travel widely. In the 1960s and 1970s her sculptures were mainly carved from wood, using forms inspired by basic rhythmic forms and structures, with each element forming a balanced whole. Her prints from this time also explore these modulations, as in the etchings Set of Eight (1975; see 1995 exh. cat., pp. 24 and 28), which consist of simple patterns of blocks and lines.^^An impression of this print is in the collection of the Tate Gallery, London. References:  M. Gooding, Kim Lim (exh. cat.). London, Waddington Gals, 1990.  AND Kim Lim: Sculpture and Works on Paper (exh. cat.). Wakefield: Yorks Sculp. Park, 1995.  AND I. King, ed. Kim Lim (exh. cat.). London, Camden A. Cent., 1999."
  },
