  {
    "Title": "Chinese Dao Coin with ancient script",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Beeghly Library Archives",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Daily Life; Currency",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1th century, 1th century, Han",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "2044 w x 552 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2569",
    "filename": [
      "2569-chinese-dao-coin-with-ancient-script.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Dao are early coins made in the shape of weapons, datable to between the first century BCE to the first century CE. Material: cast metal alloy. Size: 11 X 2.2 cm. Contains script that appears to be a descendant of oracle bone script, the earliest form of Chinese writing. This is an example of early coinage that was issued while the economy evolved from a barter to an monetary economy. Bronze knives and bronze spades were common barter items in ancient China, but a bit awkward or hazardous to carry around to trade. Some of China's first coins were made to look like a knife or like a stylized spade, so that people would think of them as money, however they were too thin and fragile to be used for anything but money. The knife coin and the spade coin developed in different areas of China about the same time. This knife coin is called the \"\"Ming\"\" after the city where it was made (not the dynasty that was much later).^^For additional information on these early Chinese coins, see: http://www.calgarycoin.com/reference/china/china2.htm"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Dao Coin with Circular Top",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Beeghly Library Archives",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Currency; Daily Life",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1th century, 1th century, Han",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "840 w x 648 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2568",
    "filename": [
      "2568-dao-coin-with-circular-top.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Dao are early coins made in the shape of weapons, datable to the 1st c BCE to the 1st century CE. Material: cast metal alloy. 7.5 cm in total length; handle is 1.2 cm in width and the circular top is 2.6 cm in width. This is an example of early coinage that was issued while the economy evolved from a barter to an monetary economy. Bronze knives and bronze spades were common barter items in ancient China, but a bit awkward or hazardous to carry around to trade. Some of China's first coins were made to look like a knife or like a stylized spade, so that people would think of them as money, however they were too thin and fragile to be used for anything but money. The knife coin and the spade coin developed in different areas of China about the same time. This knife coin is called the \"Ming\" after the city where it was made (not the dynasty that was much later).^^For additional information on these early Chinese coins, see: http://www.calgarycoin.com/reference/china/china2.htm"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Chinese table - dragon detail",
    "Creator": "Designed by Carl F. Kupfer, and made by Chinese students at Chinkiang Institute.",
    "Contributor": "Beeghly Library Archives",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Decorative Arts",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "2288 w x 1712 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2567",
    "filename": [
      "2567-chinese-table-dragon-detail.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Size: Height: 78.5 cm, length of the table-top: 118.5 cm, width of the table top: 71 cm. Material: Teakwood, Black Lacquer, and Mother of Pearl. The table was designed by Carl F. Kupfer. It was made of teakwood, black lacquer, and mother of pearl by Chinese students at Chinkiang Institute (a mission station) in 1899. No nails were used in its construction. The table is similar to one located in the Baldwin Wallace College Chapel. Both tables have the bishops??? likenesses and Chinese motifs. In the central rectangle of this table, there are figures representing the Board of Bishops of the Methodist Episcopal Church; at the corners of the rectangle, four small squares contain the likenesses of S. L. Baldwin, W. T. Smith, A. B. Leonard, and A. L. Palmer.^^Donated in 1977. The table was originally presented to an institution in Cincinnati and has been part of the collection of the East Ohio Conference Archives since 1977, and housed at OWU since 2003."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Chinese table (Board of Bishops detail)",
    "Creator": "Designed by Carl F. Kupfer, and made by Chinese students at Chinkiang Institute.",
    "Contributor": "Beeghly Library Archives",
    "Institution": "Ohio Wesleyan University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Decorative Arts; Historical Figures",
    "Language": "Chinese",
    "Country": "China",
