  {
    "Title": "Jali Screen - detail of carving",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Mills College Art Museum",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Architecture",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "India",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "18th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2553",
    "filename": [
      "2553-jali-screen-detail-of-carving.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Mottled red sandstone; 43.25 x 26 inches. This crisply carved richly ornamental panel served as an architectural element in a palace or pavilion wall space. This detail shows the scrolling floral arabesques and arched motif in the upper section of the lattice window panel are typical Mogul decorative flourishes that are common in grand residential structures throughout Mogul India.\n\nGift of William K. Ehrenfeld, PhD"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Dragon Robe (Ji Fu)",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Mills College Art Museum",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Textiles; Clothing; Government",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "18th century, Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2552",
    "filename": [
      "2552-dragon-robe-ji-fu.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Silk. This elaborately worked robe was the requisite attire for formal court affairs and is decorated in gold couched threads and multi-colored counted stitches on both sides showing nine five-clawed writhing dragons confronting flaming pearls amongst cloud scrolls and various auspicious symbols, with bats and formal pendants on a blue ground. The lower section and the sleeve ends show golden earth and water symbolism of crashing waves against rockwork outcroppings.\n\nGift of the Kroll Family"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Vishnu and Sridevi",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Mills College Art Museum",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
