  {
    "Title": "Tea Cup with Metal Cover and Stand",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "DePauw University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "19th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2447",
    "filename": [
      "2447-tea-cup-with-metal-cover-and-stand.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Teacup: 2-1/2 (H) x 3-1/2 (Dia); stand: 2-1/2 (H) x 4-1/8 (Dia); cover: 2-1/4 (H) x 3-3/8(Dia) in inches. The porcelain tea cup is decorated with five Buddhist lion dog puppies in yellow aubergine, blue and red.  It sits into a footed silver repousse stand and is fitted with a stupa-design domed matching silver cover.  The lotus shaped stand has the Eight sacred Buddhist symbols on a leaf scroll background and three lotus flowers on the high foot.  The matching lid is toped with a jade bead.  There is a illegible red mark on the base of the cup."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Water Pot to Feed the Hungry Ghosts",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "DePauw University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "19th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2446",
    "filename": [
      "2446-water-pot-to-feed-the-hungry-ghosts.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Basin (b): 9-5/8 (Dia.) x 2-3/4 (H); stand(a): 9-3/4 (H) x 7-3/8 (Dia.); pot (c): 4-1/8 (H) x 6-7/8 (Dia.) plus spout; dish (d): 5 (Dia.) x 3/4 (H) in inches. Silver-  a) Water Pot - Round covered silver pot with applied spout and side ring handle, engraved with scrolling peony design and applied Buddhist emblems.  The flat, stepped lid has a scene in the enter of a seated monk holding rosary beads and performing the \"Feeding the Hungry Ghost\" ceremony.  It is framed by a circle of Taoist Immortal symbols with leaf scrolls on the sloping sides and applied Buddhist symbols.  On the base are four unidentified stamped marks around a center indented hole.  b) Dish - Flat silver dish with double silver thread around the flaring rim.  In the center is set a small turquoise bead.  c) Stand - Folding hinged silver stand in the form of the Chinese character SHOU, long life.  d) Basin - Silver Bowl engraved with floral designs around the exterior and eight applied Buddhist symbols attached with rivets to the inside and one ring handle on the side.The bottom engraved with The Four Friends: an elephant with a monkey, hare and partridge on his back.  It has the same stamped marks ont the bottom as the water pot."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Kadampa Stupa",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "DePauw University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Sculpture; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "13th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2445",
    "filename": [
      "2445-kadampa-stupa.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "7-1/8 (H) x 3-3/4 (Circumference) inches. The stupa or chorten, is also a reliquary object and represents Buddhas enlightened mind.  The bell-shaped cavity would hold the sacred reliquary.  The bell-shaped base is surmounted a square base and thirteen tiered graduated discs with a flat lotus umbrella and lotus finial.    The base is wrapped with red and black leather.  Terese Bartholomew believes that the stupa was probably opened and resealed in this way."
  },
