  {
    "Title": "Padmasambhava (Thangka)",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "Emison Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Depauw University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Painting; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2421",
    "filename": [
      "2421-padmasambhava-thangka.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is a colored pencil drawing representing a thangka form, 23-1/2 (L) x 18 (W) inches.  The Indian guru Padmasambhava is shown in the top center.  Below to his left is his patron, the 8th century Tibetan king Trisong Detsen who invited him to spread Buddhist Dharma in Tibet.  To his lower right is the Khembo (abbot) Shay-Wa-Tsen, who mediated between Padmasambhava and the king.  A pair of crossed thunderbolts (vajras) ae located in the center of the composition.  The snowy Himalayas and a clear blue sky are shown behind them.  This image is also very skillfully rendered by an artist with prior training in the making of thangka paintings."
  },
