  {
    "Title": "Samsara (Wheel of rebirth) (Thangka)",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "Emison Art Museum",
    "Institution": "Depauw University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Painting; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2405",
    "filename": [
      "2405-samsara-wheel-of-rebirth-thangka.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Ground mineral pigment on cotton, silk, 64 (L) x 39 (W)  inches.  This is a common Tibetan theme that represents a dynamic vision of karma and the cycles of rebirth in various forms, both coarse and subtle, earthly, heavenly and hellish.  All are driven by the beastly desires symbolized by the snake, bird and pig located at the center of the wheel.  The twelve causes of rebirth are shown on the rim while the six conditions of birth are shown inside the wheel.  The wheel is held in the grasp of Yama, the lord of death.  The message here is that the teachings of the Buddha offer a path to escape from this world of endless change and suffering."
  },
