    ],
    "description": "26 inches high by 10.8 inches wide. Image of the Buddhist goddess Tara seated on a double lotus; traces of polychrome paint & furnished w/ elaborate earrings, necklace & bracelet of repousee copper; arrested termite damage most notably on hands and base; previous catalog number \"484\" on bottom of base.^^Gift of Mr. & Mrs. H. Robert Slusser."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Kubera",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "Slusser Collection",
    "Institution": "Berea College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Sculpture; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Nepal",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "19th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2150",
    "filename": [
      "2150-kubera.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Dark, red sandstone artifact from Madhya Pradesh. 6 x 4.5 x 3 inches. One of the guardians of the eight directions, Kubera belongs to a class of ancient folk deities called yakshas, who were adopted by Buddhism, Jainism, and Hinduism. He represents the power of monetary wealth. He conventionally holds a bowl or pomegranate in his right hand and a moneybag in his left. A mongoose that has the power to vomit a wish-fulfilling gem normally accompanies him. This is a fine quality image that was originally made as an architectural detail for a Hindu temple.^^Estate of Milton E. Flower."
  },
