  {
    "Title": "Japanese four-fold screen",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian  Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Painting",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "18th century, Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2313",
    "filename": [
      "2313-japanese-four-fold-screen.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Gold leaf and paint.  Depicts court ladies enjoying an outing in a summertime landscape.  Painted in the Heian style, possibly illustrating a scene from the Tales of Genji.  The details are focused on the all-important social groups (as many anthropologists have argued, it has been historically the small, face-to-face group--not the individual--that is the elemental unit of Japanese society) and the elegance of the courtly textile fashions represented."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Bronze Dalai Lama",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian  Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2312",
    "filename": [
      "2312-bronze-dalai-lama.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This small bronze is identified in the records as the Dalai Lama.  It is indeed a Lama, however it is not clear that it is one of the Dalai Lamas.  The image is gilded, sitting a lalit asana (a posture of ?ǣroyal ease?ǥ).  His short-crop of hair is painted brown/black.  He holds a manuscript with both hands.  He sits on a simple box-like throne. The base conceals ritual deposits beneath a hammered copper cap with a painted crossed vajra design."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Mahakala Thanka",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian  Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "18th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2311",
    "filename": [
      "2311-mahakala-thanka.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Gold line on red background.Made for ritual purposes.  An almost identical thanka is identified as Begtse Mahakala, Central Tibet, 18th century, in Desire and Devotion (P. Pal, 2001).  It is best examined with a magnifying glass, as its true glory is seen in the fine details and the exquisite subtlety of the modeling of the main figure."
  },
