  {
    "Title": "One of group of six Balinese paintings of women",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian  Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Painting",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Bali, Indonesia",
    "Region": "Southeast Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "20th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2297",
    "filename": [
      "2297-one-of-group-of-six-balinese-paintings-of-women.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Six virtually identical paintings varying slightly in size depicting four bare breasted Balinese women surrounding a fifth operating a traditional loom ( a skill important to her desirability as a wife).  This set is illustrative of a mode of routine production in which Balinese culture is transformed into a marketable commodity and a way of making a living in the developing cash economy of 20th century Indonesia. Dickinson accesssioned items 1985.8.41.11 and 12  are similar to the other six, but depict other idealized female activities: applying cosmetics, dressing and making flower ornaments."
  },
