  {
    "Title": "Blue Jambhala",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "Dickinson College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Sculpture; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "17th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1194 w x 1812 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2152",
    "filename": [
      "2152-blue-jambhala.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Blue Jambhala in his wrathful aspect, associated with the Aksobhya Buddhas, with mongoose in left hand, skull bowl in right, crowned by Aksobhya, standing on Kubera, who vomits jewels. A pot full of jewels sits in the deity's lotus throne. Jambhala, who appears in several colors and iconographic forms, is a deity for wealth,  prosperity, and success. (De Mallman notes that Jambhala is sometimes included in the Ratnasambhava class)"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Figure of a monk",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "Dickinson College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Nepal",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "17th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1320 w x 1806 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2151",
    "filename": [
      "2151-figure-of-a-monk.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Nepalese brass artifact of a seated monk. The robes & begging bowl indicate a monk.  His head is shaved in the front, but three long strands of hair cascade down his back.  His robe displays Chinese designs only visible from the back.  He holds a vajra in his right hand and appears to wear earrings. The left earring is inlaid with what appears to be red sealing wax while the one on the right bears traces of the same material. The base conceals ritual deposits beneath a hammered copper cap marked with a crossed vajra design.  All of these metal images were originally made for ritual use.  The containers for deposits hidden within the bases indicate a category of images once valued for their efficacy.  It is instructive to consider what their value is in their present situation, surrounded as they are by a society that may appreciate their visible surfaces, and yet generally dismisses the idea that images such as these can exercise power when skillfully utilized."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Kubera",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "Dickinson College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "India",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "9th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1404 w x 1854 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2318",
    "filename": [
      "2318-kubera.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "12.6 inches high x 7 inches wide. Image of Hindu/Buddhist deity Kubera displaying the wealth-emitting mongoose in his left hand; copper; previous catalog number \"157\" on inside of base. Also known as Jambhala in Tibetan.^^Gift of Mr. & Mrs. H. Robert Slusser."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Stucco head of Buddhist divinity",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Dickinson College. Trout Gallery",
    "Institution": "Dickinson College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Gandhara, India",
    "Region": "South Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "5th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1578 w x 2208 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2149",
    "filename": [
      "2149-stucco-head-of-buddhist-divinity.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This probably comes from a site (Hadda or Taxila) in present day Afghanistan. These pieces are noteworthy for their great antiquity. They display characteristics of a late Hellenistic visual language synthesized with that of contemporary northern India. Aside from their obvious historical and art historical significance, they could also be useful in broader academic discussions of processes we now call globalization in antiquity."
  },
