  {
    "Title": "The Window, front view",
    "Creator": "Kawanishi Hide",
    "Contributor": "Special Collections, Charles E. Shain Library. Connecticut College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2105",
    "filename": [
      "2105-the-window-front-view.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "In contrast to Kawanishi's view of the industrial port of Kobe, this print represents the modern-looking traditional side of Japan, with starkly vertical and horizontal lines of shoji screens opened just enough to reveal a stone lantern and garden greenery.  This work is a charming example of how sosaku hanga could emphasize the apparent paradox of the inherent modernity of traditional Japan.  Kawanishi is associated with the Sosaku hanga (creative printmaking) movement which flourished after the end of the Pacific War.  Unlike the Shin-hanga artists, who sold their designs to publishers employing professional specialist woodblock carvers and printers, Sosaku hanga artists took direct control of all aspects of printmaking, carving their own blocks and personally inking and printing their works."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Port in Early Dawn, front view",
    "Creator": "Kawanishi Hide",
    "Contributor": "Special Collections, Charles E. Shain Library. Connecticut College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2104",
    "filename": [
      "2104-port-in-early-dawn-front-view.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Another representation of Kawanishi???s home town, with a glowing buoy setting off the distant lighthouse and dark mountain, this print is a good example of the style.\n\nKawanishi is associated with the Sosaku hanga (creative printmaking) movement which flourished after the end of the Pacific War.  Unlike the Shin-hanga artists, who sold their designs to publishers employing professional specialist woodblock carvers and printers, Sosaku hanga artists took direct control of all aspects of printmaking, carving their own blocks and personally inking and printing their works."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ainu, front view",
    "Creator": "Kawanishi Hide",
    "Contributor": "Special Collections, Charles E. Shain Library. Connecticut College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Prints",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2103",
    "filename": [
      "2103-ainu-front-view.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This modernist portrait of a representative of Japan's northern aborigine minority, dressed for a dance performance, is a fine example of figurative printmaking in the sosaku hanga mode.\n\nKawanishi is associated with the Sosaku hanga (creative printmaking) movement which flourished after the end of the Pacific War.  Unlike the Shin-hanga artists, who sold their designs to publishers employing professional specialist woodblock carvers and printers, S?saku hanga artists took direct control of all aspects of printmaking, carving their own blocks and personally inking and printing their works."
  },
