  {
    "Title": "Three unserved Tanzaku prints (poem cards)",
    "Creator": "Utagawa Hiroshige",
    "Contributor": "The Wriston Art Gallery. Lawrence University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian\nArt in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college reta",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2011",
    "filename": [
      "2011-three-unserved-tanzaku-prints-poem-cards.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Right: Girl Playing with a Battledore in the New Year\nCenter: A Court Lady on an Outing for Picking herbs in the New Year\nLeft: Paper Hina Dolls and a Peach Branch\n\nOne of the most well known 19th century ukiyo-e artists, famous for his landscape views, particularly his images of the Tokaido.\n\nMost famous for his series of views along the Tokaido and of Edo and its surrounds, Hiroshige was also a prolific artist with a variety of subject matters. This sheet of three poem cards would have been cut into three separate prints.  \n\n"
  },
  {
    "Title": "Overspread view from Kasumigaseki street",
    "Creator": "Utagawa Hiroshige",
    "Contributor": "The Wriston Art Gallery. Lawrence University",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian\nArt in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college reta",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "Punjabi",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "2010",
    "filename": [
      "2010-overspread-view-from-kasumigaseki-street.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "From the Famous Places of Edo series.\n\nOne of the most well known 19th century ukiyo-e artists, famous for his landscape views, particularly his images of the Tokaido.\n\nKasumigaseki Street was part of the quarter inhabited by Samurai and retainers. During almost the entire Edo period, Samurai, no longer needed to wage wars sought official posts in and around Edo. Their large numbers, relative economic independence, and surfeit of time made the pleasure quarters of Edo a bustling, dynamic center of Kabuki theater, brothels, and teahouses.  \n\n"
  },
