  {
    "Title": "Carved figure (side)",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Fine Arts Collection. Luther College.",
    "Institution": "Luther College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "Edo-Tokugawa",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "1136 w x 1950 h, 300 ppi",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1935",
    "filename": [
      "1935-carved-figure-side.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Netsuke, a small accessory, functioned as a toggle or button for the wearing of articles, such as a pouch or a purse, on a sash, or obi in Japanese, in traditional Japanese clothing (kimono). This netsuke features a Buddhist figure, probably Pu-tai (in Chinese) who is the Buddha of Happiness and Fortune and is usually portrayed as a chubby monk in an open-chested robe.  This image became popular due to his association with folk myths in both China and Japan.  The signature at the bottom does not indicate the carver, but the name of the examiner, which is unfortunately unidentifiable. "
  },
