    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1855",
    "filename": [
      "1855-grave-with-torii.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This old grave site has a large traditional stone and the space is nicely framed by a Shinto torii. This kind of complex shows how Buddhist and Shinto forms merge easily in Japanese sensibility."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Classical grave stone",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Buddhism; Sculpture",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.216667",
    "Longitude": "135.583333",
    "Date": "07/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1854",
    "filename": [
      "1854-classical-grave-stone.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This shape is common to the Shingon school of Japanese Buddhism and reflects its doctrines. The five geometric shapes of this stupa-like grave stone signify the five elements shared by all living things (from bottom to top, cube, sphere, pyramid, hemisphere, drop): earth, water, fire, air and space. Each of these elements has its own \"seed\" syllable or mantra, that is carved into the stone here in its Sanskrit form."
  },
