  {
    "Title": "Streetside stall selling religious decorations",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Buddhism; Shinto; Stores",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.216667",
    "Longitude": "135.583333",
    "Date": "11/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1826",
    "filename": [
      "1826-streetside-stall-selling-religious-decorations.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Many such stalls in Koyasan sell evergreen fronds to people for embellishing their family altars at home where ancestors are revered. This one is in a spot very characteristic of Koyasan: the old stone wall behind and the line of toriis heading up a path to the left bespeak the charm of this old mountain town (founded in the early 9th century) with its limitless reminders of traditional religion."
  },
