    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1803",
    "filename": [
      "1803-ikuta-jinja-fortunes-tied-onto-branches.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Just outside the main entrance gate is a makeshift tree (constructed because the natural tree was full!) of long, thin hanging wooden dowels, on which many white paper fortune strips (mikuji) are folded."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ikuta Jinja - Shrine souveniers and amulets",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Shinto; Ritual; Adults; Stores",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.666667",
    "Longitude": "135.116667",
    "Date": "10/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1802",
    "filename": [
      "1802-ikuta-jinja-shrine-souveniers-and-amulets.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This young woman works in a stall that sells various types of amulets (o-mamori). Many Japanese visitors will purchase one when they visit a major shrine such as Ikuta Jinja. They will often keep it near them until their next visit (and purchase), in places such as in their purse, tied onto a back pack, or hanging from a car mirror."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ikuta Jinja - Amulets",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Shinto; Ritual",
    "Language": "Japanese",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.666667",
    "Longitude": "135.116667",
    "Date": "10/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1801",
    "filename": [
      "1801-ikuta-jinja-amulets.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Most of the amulets (o-mamori) shown here are for success in academics, either for good grades or for passing an entrance exam into the school of your choice. The prices here, which are more or less standard, range from 500 to 1000 yen (from $4-$8)."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ikuta Jinja - Shrine fortune telling",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Shinto; Ritual",
    "Language": "Japanese",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.666667",
    "Longitude": "135.116667",
    "Date": "10/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1800",
    "filename": [
      "1800-ikuta-jinja-shrine-fortune-telling.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is a \"mikujiori\" box, from which one draws a paper packet in which is written a fortune. The fortune is printed on a small piece of paper and, if it is auspicious, a visitor will usually fold it into a long, thin strip and then tie it around a small branch of a tree in the shrine compound. It is as if this act also ties a bond between one's future and the deity of the temple: one wishes that the kami will help fulfill your good fortune. If the fortune does not bode well, the visitor has the option of taking another mikuji (which usually costs less -- this box says, \"first fortune 200 yen,\" a little under $2)."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Ikuta Jinja - Circle of rope leading to main shrine building",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
