    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Shinto",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.666667",
    "Longitude": "135.116667",
    "Date": "10/07/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1794",
    "filename": [
      "1794-ikuta-jinja-inner-sanctuary.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is the view of the interior of the shrine from just to the left of where worshippers deposit coins, ring the bell, clap and bow. Beyond the courtyard-like space, bathed in sunlight in this photo, back in the shade is a structure that houses the symbols of the deity honored here.  Shinto shrines rarely have an indoor space for worship. The structures are built to demarcate and embellish the area but usually not to contain worship indoors."
  },
  {
