    "filename": [
      "1731-hasedera-alongside-covered-stairway.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This is the view of the perimeter of the stairs leading to the main temple visible above. The terraces and the rain gutter are made of hand-placed stone. Note the small stone bridges apparently designed for access to the plants across the gutter."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hasedera - Monks walking  up  stairs",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Buddhism; Adults",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.516667",
    "Longitude": "135.883333",
    "Date": "30/06/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1730",
    "filename": [
      "1730-hasedera-monks-walking-up-stairs.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Hasedera is an active training ground for Shingon Buddhist priests, who can be seen moving about the complex. Their prayers can often be heard resounding within many of the temple buildings, in which groups will chant in a hauntingly beautiful traditional manner."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Hasedera - Corridor stairs leading to main temple",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Gardiner, David L. , 1957-",
    "Institution": "Colorado College",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.  Acknowledgment to be given to the IDEAS image database and the photographer.  Photographer retains copyright.",
    "Subject": "Sites; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "34.516667",
    "Longitude": "135.883333",
    "Date": "30/06/2002",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "640 w x 480 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Japanese Religion Images",
    "id": "1729",
    "filename": [
      "1729-hasedera-corridor-stairs-leading-to-main-temple.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This lovely covered stairway (nobori-ro) originally dates from 1039 but was reconstructed in the Meiji period. The stone lanterns and flowering shrubs on both sides make for an exquisite ascending garden, while at night the spherical lamps above cast a fine glow. The pillar on the left says that this is a place where heavenly deities reside, which is a Shinto-esque reference, while one not visible to its right states that Buddhas also are active here."
  },
