  {
    "Title": "Vishnu",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "St. Olaf College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Hinduism; Figures; Painting; Poster",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "India",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "2549",
    "filename": [
      "2549-vishnu.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Copper alloy; 20.5 inches in height. Vishnu is one of the three major deities in Hinduism and is thought to be the preserver of the universe. Here he is shown with his attributes of the conch shell ('sankha') and the wheel ('cakra') in two of his four hands, his dhoti neatly pleated and with meditation cords around his waist. His crown is typically cylindrical and surmounted with a bud-shaped finial and bordered with triangular foliate medallions. Created during the Vijayanagar period (1350-1565 CE), the god is posed to be carried by the temple priests in a parade-like procession through the streets."
  },
