  {
    "Title": "Kyogen",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Nipponia, no. 22, 2002, p. 11.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Theater; Literature; Traditional Theater",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "779 w x 665 h, 150 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "1518",
    "filename": [
      "1518-kyogen.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Top:  Shigeyama Motohiko (left) and his brother Ippei in a short kyogen play.  Kyogen is Japan's oldest form of spoken drama.  It is closely associated with the Noh theater and was originally performed during the intervals between the acts of a Noh play.  While Noh themes are often serious, kyogen presents a comical  view of everyday situations, making it easier to enjoy.    Lower:  The folding fan : an important prop on the kyogen stage."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Noh Mask: Hannya",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Woudenberg, Annie",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Pictorial Encyclopedia of Japanese Culture Tokyo: Gakken Co., Ltd, 1987, scan of image on p.43.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Theater; Sculpture; Material Culture; Traditional theatre",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1392-1573",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "533 w x 768 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "1517",
    "filename": [
      "1517-noh-mask-hannya.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The Hannya \"devil\" mask expresses the violent anger and distress of a woman whose love and trust have been betrayed, turning her into a raging, revengeful female demon.  The two horns protruding from disorderly hair evince diabolic malevolence, and the upper lip, tense and pointed in the center like a snake's, and the glinting of the  metal eyes and teeth effectively add to her menace.  This mask is attributed to the monk Hannya, who is said to have lived in Nara during the Muromachi period and to have originated this type of mask.   A lesser form of the hannya mask is the Namanari mask."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Heroic Mountain God",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Annie Woudenberg",
    "Institution": "St. Olaf College",
    "Source": "Nipponia, no. 22, 2002, p. 7.",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Theater; Artists",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Japan",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "526 w x 768 h, 72 ppi",
    "Collection": "Asian Takeout",
    "id": "1516",
    "filename": [
      "1516-heroic-mountain-god.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Nakamura Kantaro, a 20 year old kabuki actor, plays a heroic mountain god with supernatural powers in Momiji-gari.  Kabuki, Japan's most famous classical theatre, has a history of about 400 years.  It began as a women's dance routine (kabuki odori), but soon evolved into stage plays, with  men taking all of the acting roles.  Kabuki acting techniques are passed from father to son, and so techniques tend to remain within a limited number of acting families.  Each family becomes the custodian of certain acting roles, and these roles, too, are passed from one generation to the next."
  },
