      "1260-large-porcelain-dish-detail-of-bottom.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "The decoration on this blue and white charger was inspired by Islamic ceramics of the 16th and 17th centuries and influenced the decorative patterns used on 18th century Dutch Delft wares.^^Gift of Dr. William Courtney Douglass."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Deep porcelain bowl - side view",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "College of Wooster. Art Museum",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1662-1722, Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1259",
    "filename": [
      "1259-deep-porcelain-bowl-side-view.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "This blue and white decorated porcelain bowl is an example of the popular bird and flower decorative motif of the early Qing Dynasty.  Made at the Imperial porcelain factories at Jingtezhen in Jiangxi Provence, this bowl is inscribed with the reign mark of the Emperor Kangxi. These porcelain factories flourished during the Ming Dynasty but their output declined in quantity and quality immediately after the Manchu invasion. Under Kangxi???s patronage porcelain manufacture once again flourished and for the next 130 years some of the most exquisite porcelains were created to the delight of three successive Emperors. 8 1/2 inches in diameter.^^Gift of William Kelly."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Bronze censor  - back view",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "College of Wooster. Art Museum",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Decorative Arts; Sculpture; Daoism; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "1644-1912, Qing",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1258",
    "filename": [
      "1258-bronze-censor-back-view.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Cast in the form of the mythical three-legged toad, emblematic of the unattainable and great wealth, the upper portion of its body forms the cover and the three spread legs, the base. Its mouth is pierced to allow the incense to drift upwards. This beast has both Taoist and Buddhist implications, as do many of legendary beasts and creatures in traditional Chinese iconography.  8 inches long, 5 inches high.^^Gift of William Kelly."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Lacquer tray - top view",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "College of Wooster. Art Museum",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
