  {
    "Title": "Limepot",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lake Forest College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Vietnam",
    "Region": "Southeast Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "17th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1127",
    "filename": [
      "1127-limepot.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Stoneware, H: 5 1/2\" x Dia: 4\". Glazed wares (wood-ash glazed) were first produced in Southeast Asia in Vietnam during the first century; the technique was undoubtedly a legacy of the Chinese, who ruled Vietnam from 111 BCE to 979 CE. Vietnamese ceramics relate to Chinese ceramics in terms of glazes, shapes, but differences in the clay body (Vietnamese wares have a white clay body), decoration, and the fact the Vietnamese produced only stoneware, set them apart.   This limepot is an example of a unique shape that is not found in Chinese wares, but instead points to a distinctly Southeast Asian usage. Certain cultural traits set the region of Southeast Asia apart from Chinese or Indian cultures. These traits include betel chewing, cockfighting, houses on stilts, piston bellows, musical pattern dominated by gongs, similar patterns of body decoration, the concept of spirit or soul stuff, and the prominence of women in descent, ritual matters, market and agriculture.   Betel chewing involves creating a quid by wrapping slaked lime and cut areca nuts in a betel leaf; the effect is that of a mild aphrodisiac. Limepots of this type were created in all sizes and in a variety of glazes. As in this example, the handle is interpreted to look like a vine that ends in leaves, often of the areca plant.^^Gift of Nancy Moorhead, class of 1980. "
  },
  {
    "Title": "Limepot - bottom view",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lake Forest College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Vietnam",
    "Region": "Southeast Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "17th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1126",
    "filename": [
      "1126-limepot-bottom-view.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Stoneware, H: 5 1/2\" x Dia: 4\". Glazed wares (wood-ash glazed) were first produced in Southeast Asia in Vietnam during the first century; the technique was undoubtedly a legacy of the Chinese, who ruled Vietnam from 111 BCE to 979 CE. Vietnamese ceramics relate to Chinese ceramics in terms of glazes, shapes, but differences in the clay body (Vietnamese wares have a white clay body), decoration, and the fact the Vietnamese produced only stoneware, set them apart.   This limepot is an example of a unique shape that is not found in Chinese wares, but instead points to a distinctly Southeast Asian usage. Certain cultural traits set the region of Southeast Asia apart from Chinese or Indian cultures. These traits include betel chewing, cockfighting, houses on stilts, piston bellows, musical pattern dominated by gongs, similar patterns of body decoration, the concept of spirit or soul stuff, and the prominence of women in descent, ritual matters, market and agriculture.   Betel chewing involves creating a quid by wrapping slaked lime and cut areca nuts in a betel leaf; the effect is that of a mild aphrodisiac. Limepots of this type were created in all sizes and in a variety of glazes. As in this example, the handle is interpreted to look like a vine that ends in leaves, often of the areca plant.^^Gift of Nancy Moorhead, class of 1980."
  },
  {
    "Title": "KENDI, Sawankhalok style",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lake Forest College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Ceramics",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Thailand",
    "Region": "",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1125",
    "filename": [
      "1125-kendi-sawankhalok-style.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "H: 6 5/8\" x Dia: 8\". Buddhist and Hindu rituals in Southeast Asia required the lustration of images, and the kendi, one of the larger vessels used, is depicted in sculptural reliefs as early as the ninth century. This modern kendi is constructed in the typical mammiform shape of the Southeast Asian kendi. Although this piece is modern, it illustrates both an important type of vessel with its links to both Buddhist and Hindu ritual and decorative techniques used in the production of Thai ceramics.    The potter has incised the vessel in an overall, unidentifiable floral motif, then used an iron brown glaze to fill the design and a wood-ash white glaze for the background. The incised lines serve to separate the two glazes, so that they don't run into each other. In this particular instance, those lines, freshly cut and without any evidence of aging, indicate the vessel is modern."
  },
  {
    "Title": "KENDI, Sawankhalok style - bottom view",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Lake Forest College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
