  {
    "Title": "Two Birds in a Tree - detail",
    "Creator": "",
    "Contributor": "Canisius 300",
    "Institution": "Fairfield University",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only. Acknowledgement to be given to the ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project and to the college from whose collection the work comes. The individual college retains copyright to the work.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Painting",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "China",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "20th century",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "1078",
    "filename": [
      "1078-two-birds-in-a-tree-detail.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "Ink and color on paper. 4 ft x 2 ft.  This painting likely was intended to allude to conventional themes in China: particular birds (and ducks) with their own specific iconography, while the flowering trees allude the seasonal themes.  Although the brushwork style here is dramatically different from its predecessors, 'bird and flower' paintings, or 'fur and feather' paintings,  are a tradition which can be traced back to the Northern Song dynasty (960-1127).  Comparing this painting to examples from the Song dynasty and later would provide students with a succinct exercise in understanding developments in Chinese painting, both in terms of continuities and innovation.^^Bequest of Farrell Estate"
  },
