  {
    "Title": "Traveling Shrine Charm Box or gao - detail of tsa tsa inside",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "Berea College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
    "Rights": "Materials may be used for educational, non-commercial purposes only.",
    "Subject": "Arts; Sculpture; Buddhism",
    "Language": "",
    "Country": "Tibet",
    "Region": "East Asia",
    "Latitude": "",
    "Longitude": "",
    "Date": "",
    "Coverage": "",
    "Type": "Still Image",
    "Dimensions": "",
    "Collection": "ASIANetwork-Luce Asian Art in the Undergraduate Curriculum Project",
    "id": "904",
    "filename": [
      "904-traveling-shrine-charm-box-or-gao-detail-of-tsa-ts.jpg"
    ],
    "description": "3 5/8 by 3 5/8 inches; mixed metal.  A \"gao\" is a reliquary, most often worn around the neck. Some are elaborate and high art pieces, with silver, gold, and jewels. Charm or amulet inside referred to as a 'tsa tsa'. A \"tsa tsa\" is sometimes included in a gao, and also in stupas in multiples, sometimes hundreds. It is made of clay, pressed by a mold, of a Buddha, bodhisattva, other deities, emblems, or prayers. Some tsa tsa have pieces of blessed grain or other stuff mixed with the clay; these materials have religious power and makes the gao effective against harm, attack by spirits or humans, against disease, etc.    Acquired by Berea College in 1973.^^Gift of Lucy Titcomb."
  },
  {
    "Title": "Traveling shrine charm box or 'gao'",
    "Creator": "Unknown",
    "Contributor": "Berea College",
    "Institution": "",
    "Source": "",
